cid, diammonium salt\n  * sulphate of ammonia\nDefinition\n    An inorganic sulfate salt obtained by reaction of sulfuric acid with two equivalents of ammonia. A high-melting (decomposes above 280°C) white solid which is very soluble in water (70.6 g/100 g water at 0°C; 103.8 g/100 g water at 100°C), it is widely used as a fertilizer for alkaline soils.\n\n  * cas:7783-20-2\n  * kegg.drug:D08853\n  * metacyc.compound:NH42SO4\n  * ppdb:36\n  * pubmed:20556652\n  * reaxys:11343144\n  * wikipedia.en:Ammonium_sulfate\nOntology\n    ChEBI  ( EBI )\nResources\n    CTD\n\nRelationships\n\nPhenotype\n\nPhenotype resulting from ammonium sulfate\n\nPhenotype where environments contain ammonium sulfate\n\nPhenotype modified by environments containing ammonium sulfate\n\nPhenotype affecting ammonium sulfate--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEcology of methanogenic and methane-oxidizing microorganisms in paddy soil ecosystem (https://doi.org/10.1080/00380768.2021.1953355)\nciteturn30search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology 51: 1663–1669. doi:10.1099/00207713-51-5-1663.\n(Open in a new window)PubMed (Open in a new window)Web of Science ®(Open in a new window)Google Scholar\n\n  * Dianou, D., . T., S. Miyaki, H. Asakawa, K. Morii, H. Nagaoka, H. Oyaizu, and S. Matsumoto. 2001. “Methanoculleus chikugoensis sp. nov., A Novel Methanogenic Archaeon Isolated from a Paddy Field Soil in Japan and DNA-DNA Hybridization among Methanoculleus Species.” International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology 51: 1663–1669. doi:10.1099/00207713-51-5-1663.\n\n(Open in a new window)PubMed (Open in a new window)Web of Science ®(Open in a new window)Google Scholar\n\n  * Dubey, S. K., A. Singh, T. Watanabe, S. Asakawa, A. Singla, H. Arai, and K. Inubushi. 2014. “Methane Production Potential and Methanogenic Archaeal Community Structure in Trop